          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Iron deficiency anemia sits in the blood chapter. Which chapter would an underlying bleeding ulcer be coded from?",
          "answer": "**Short answer:** An underlying bleeding ulcer is coded from **Chapter 11, Diseases of the digestive system (K00-K95)**. Peptic ulcers sit in the block for the esophagus, stomach and duodenum (K20-K31), grouped by site: gastric, duodenal, unspecified, and gastrojejunal (*ICD-10-CM Chapter 11 overview*). My search did not return the exact K25-K28 range or the bleeding-specific ulcer codes, so I can't confirm those numbers from the source material.\n\n**Contrast with iron deficiency anemia:** Iron deficiency anemia is D50, the first code in the nutritional anemia block of Chapter 3, the blood chapter (*ICD-10-CM Chapter 3 overview*). The two codes answer different questions. The blood code names the anemia. The digestive code names the source of the bleeding.\n\n**Cause versus complication:** The Chapter 3 material says that chronic blood loss causing iron deficiency is coded from whichever chapter owns the bleeding site (*ICD-10-CM Chapter 3*). For a bleeding ulcer, that points to Chapter 11. The same passage notes that anemia often carries sequencing instructions that reach into other chapters. However, my final lookup on the general rule for underlying cause versus complication failed because the tool-call limit was reached. I can't tell you from these sources which code is listed first, so that detail should be checked against the official coding guidelines.\n\nCoding decisions depend on the full medical record and are made by trained coders and clinicians. If you or someone you know has signs of bleeding, such as black or bloody stools or vomiting blood, seek medical care promptly.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
